<spell-notebook "shape usage">
	<header level="1">
		Shape
		<header>A shape is a three dimensional object displayed on a two dimensional plane.</>
	</>
	<shape-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#invoker-commands">Invoker commands</>
				<ui-item link="#methods-and-events">Methods and events</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
				<ui-item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (sides, flips, commands, methods, events), accessibility, framework snippets. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			A shape's children are its sides: one `<ui-side>` per face, in order, as Fomantic's markup has a `.side` per
			face inside `.sides`. Only one shows at a time.

			- `active-index` (from 0) is the side shown. Changing it turns the shape the `direction` way to that side.
			- `direction` is `up`, `down`, `left` (the default), `right`, `over` or `back`. The attribute is `direction`, not
			  `flip`: `flip()` is a method.
			- `cube` gives square grey faces; `text` sides of text.
			- `duration` is the length of a turn: ms (`600`) or a CSS time (`0.6s`).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Invoker commands">
		<markdown>
			Turn a shape from a button with no script: `commandfor` is the shape's id, `command` what to do. Works on a
			native `<button>` and a `<ui-button>` alike.

			- `--next` / `--previous`: the next / previous side (both wrap round), the `direction` attribute's way.
			- `--flip-up`, `--flip-down`, `--flip-left`, `--flip-right`, `--flip-over`, `--flip-back`: the next side, THAT
			  way (Fomantic's `flip up` ... behaviours).
		</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<ui-button commandfor="dice" command="--flip-up" icon="long arrow alternate up" aria-label="Flip up"></ui-button>
			<ui-shape id="dice" cube>
			  <ui-side>1</ui-side>
			  <ui-side>2</ui-side>
			</ui-shape>
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Methods and events">
		<markdown>
			The host has Fomantic's behaviours as methods, each resolving once the turn has finished (`true`), or `false`
			when there was nothing to do:

			- `flip(direction?, index?)` -- turn `direction` (default: the attribute's) to side `index` (default: the next).
			- `next()` / `previous()` -- the next / previous side, wrapping round.

			Each writes `activeIndex`, so it reflects. Flips queue, as Fomantic's did. `ui-animated` fires once another side
			is shown (Fomantic's `onChange`), with `{ activeIndex, side, flip }`.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const dice = document.querySelector("#dice")
			await dice.next() // the next side, the `direction` way
			await dice.flip("back", 0) // back to the first side
			dice.addEventListener("ui-animated", (event) => console.log(event.detail.activeIndex))
			```
		</>
		<markdown>
			The turn is Fomantic's own geometry, as a CSS transition; under reduced motion the sides swap at once, and
			`ui-animated` fires all the same.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- Only the side shown is in the accessibility tree; the others are hidden.
			- The box that turns is a polite live region, so the new side is read out after a flip.
			- A shape has no keyboard of its own: turn it from real buttons (`commandfor`), so everyone can. Name icon-only
			  buttons with `aria-label`.
			- If the element fails to render, it shows its sides through a bare `<slot>`, with no flips, invoker
			  commands or `ui-animated`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>Bind `active-index` and listen for `ui-animated`, or call the methods through a ref.</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			<ui-shape cube active-index={side} onui-animated={(event) => setSide(event.detail.activeIndex)}>
			  ...
			</ui-shape>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-shape cube :active-index="side" @ui-animated="(event) => (side = event.detail.activeIndex)">...</ui-shape>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			// events and rich data are plain props:  `on-<event>={...}`, `options={...}`
			<ui-shape
			  cube
			  active-index={side()}
			  on-ui-animated={(event) => setSide(event.detail.activeIndex)}
			>
			  ...
			</ui-shape>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
